The most common culprit is **objects or functions being recreated on each render**, even though they look the same. Since JavaScript compares objects/functions by reference (not value), a new object means React thinks the props changed.

Here are the typical scenarios:

1. **Inline objects/functions in props:**
```js
// ❌ Bad — creates new object every render
<Child data={{ name: "John" }} />

// ✅ Good — stable reference
const data = { name: "John" };
<Child data={data} />
```

2. **Inline callbacks:**
```js
// ❌ Bad — new function every render
<Child onClick={() => handleClick()} />

// ✅ Good — use useCallback
const handleClick = useCallback(() => { /* ... */ }, []);
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

3. **Parent re-renders trigger child re-renders** — even with identical props. Use `React.memo()` to skip this:
```js
const Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {
  return <div>{data.name}</div>;
});
```

4. **Derived state or computed values** — if creating new arrays/objects derived from props:
```js
// ❌ Bad
const filteredItems = props.items.filter(...); // new array every render

// ✅ Good
const filteredItems = useMemo(() => props.items.filter(...), [props.items]);
```

**To diagnose:** Open React DevTools Profiler, enable "Highlight updates when components render", and watch which components re-render. Check the Props tab to see if they're actually identical.

